## DJ-04 · 2026-06-12 · AEGIS OS Scope Boundary

**Context:** AEGIS OS was pitched directly to VV ("Hi Vivek") as a sovereign runtime decision control layer with explicit defence scenarios (DDIL, autonomous weapons testing, intelligence admissibility). The sender appears to have a prior connection to VV.

**Decision:** Engage on commercial AI governance framing (BFSI, DPDP, enterprise) only. Any advisory input must be explicitly scoped to non-defence use cases in writing before engagement begins. Defer defence scenario discussions — electronic warfare, autonomous systems, intelligence workflows — to post-superannuation.

**Rationale:** AEGIS OS's commercial positioning as a runtime AI governance enforcement layer is directly within VV's advisory positioning and carries no serving-officer conflict. However, the defence scenarios cited in the pitch (DDIL, autonomous weapons testing, intelligence admissibility) position the product in defence AI governance. Advisory input from a serving CSO on defence AI control architectures — even framed as "non-sensitive environments" — risks being read as endorsing a specific vendor architecture for defence use.

**Constraints:** Before any engagement, obtain written scope confirmation from the AEGIS OS founder that advisory input is limited to commercial AI governance (BFSI, enterprise, DPDP) and does not extend to defence scenarios. If scope cannot be confirmed, defer entirely to post-superannuation.

**Review trigger:** Q3 2026 — if scope is confirmed in writing as commercial-only, limited advisory can begin. May 2027 (Army exit) — reassess defence scenario advisory.

**Linked note:** [[AEGIS_OS_Inbound]]

